WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.000
The first film showed PIP3 recruiting Akt to the membrane, where two phosphates

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.200
activate it. Once Akt turns on, how does one kinase drive different tissue jobs

3
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.300
without running forever?

4
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.280
The answer combines tissue-specific targets with lipid erasers and negative

5
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.280
feedback.

6
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:24.883
Start in muscle and fat cells.

7
00:00:26.123 --> 00:00:30.303
At rest, emerald glucose transporters wait inside small vesicles.

8
00:00:31.563 --> 00:00:35.283
A brake protein, AS160, holds those vesicles back.

9
00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:39.623
Active Akt phosphorylates that brake and switches it off.

10
00:00:40.843 --> 00:00:45.903
With help from other proteins, vesicles then fuse with the surface, letting glucose

11
00:00:45.903 --> 00:00:46.903
stream in.

12
00:00:47.617 --> 00:00:51.157
Next, watch glycogen storage inside muscle and liver.

13
00:00:52.517 --> 00:00:58.597
An enzyme called GSK3 normally phosphorylates glycogen synthase and keeps it slow.

14
00:01:00.057 --> 00:01:03.557
Active Akt phosphorylates GSK3 and shuts it down.

15
00:01:03.557 --> 00:01:08.957
With that brake gone, glycogen synthase speeds up and builds storage chains.

16
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.160
Now zoom into a liver nucleus.

17
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.920
Between meals, FOXO1 sits on DNA, driving genes that build new glucose.

18
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.420
Active Akt phosphorylates FOXO1.

19
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:27.540
Partner proteins then help move it out and hold it in the cytosol.

20
00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.820
Over hours, those glucose-making genes quiet down.

21
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:35.673
Notice the shared pattern across all three jobs.

22
00:01:37.173 --> 00:01:39.033
Akt switches each target protein off.

23
00:01:40.393 --> 00:01:45.393
Removing a brake turns uptake and storage up; removing a driver turns glucose-making

24
00:01:45.393 --> 00:01:48.633
genes down. Keep this second rule.

25
00:01:49.953 --> 00:01:54.693
Akt phosphorylates tissue-specific targets, while erasers and feedback limit the

26
00:01:54.693 --> 00:01:55.693
signal.

27
00:01:56.067 --> 00:01:58.007
Every signal needs an off switch.

28
00:01:59.507 --> 00:02:03.327
Tyrosine phosphatases reset the receptor and docking protein.

29
00:02:04.787 --> 00:02:10.687
Mint-green PTEN strips the 3-phosphate from PIP3, restoring slate PIP2.

30
00:02:11.887 --> 00:02:15.367
With its address gone, Akt leaves and loses both phosphates.

31
00:02:16.707 --> 00:02:23.807
Downstream kinases also mark IRS-1 on serines, making it a poorer landing pad for PI

32
00:02:23.807 --> 00:02:24.807
3-kinase.

33
00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.250
Test the pattern on one target.

34
00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:33.310
Suppose AS160 is mutated so Akt can no longer phosphorylate it.

35
00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:38.150
When insulin arrives, what happens to glucose uptake in a fat cell?

36
00:02:44.083 --> 00:02:49.543
Fewer vesicles reach the surface, so uptake rises much less, though Akt itself is

37
00:02:49.543 --> 00:02:53.183
active. Now push the whole relay both ways.

38
00:02:54.523 --> 00:03:00.563
Lose the eraser PTEN, or mutate PI 3-kinase, and growth signals lock on in tumours.

39
00:03:01.583 --> 00:03:06.343
Overdo serine feedback, and insulin resistance weakens the same relay.

40
00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:12.787
One kinase directs muscle, fat, liver, and growing cells because each tissue

41
00:03:12.787 --> 00:03:14.227
supplies its own targets.

42
00:03:15.487 --> 00:03:18.107
Lose an eraser and the signal locks on.

43
00:03:19.187 --> 00:03:22.667
Overdrive feedback and the cell responds weakly to insulin.

44
00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:29.047
Healthy signaling pairs active Akt with reliable erasers and negative feedback.
